<blockquote>
<p>I don't think the focus on 'Mr. Exolia' works for me here; particularly since it seems to rely entirely on him being humorous, which is super hard to pull off in text. In part it's also cos he doesn't really…do anything? He doesn't feel like a protagonist driving the story forward and in part that's because there's not really a story being driven forward? There's no-one here making choices that affect the outcome here and it feels….lacking as a result?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a very bare bones description I've written here to fit within the forums parameters. There is more detail to the story in which Exolia sets up some difficult decisions for the foundation site staff. Its in my sandbox if you'd like to take a look.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>There's also not really a sense of characters grow: they change somewhat throughout, but only when the story forces them to (e.g., the gambling and losing causes Exolia to become a monster, there's no personal transition from happy funny chill dude to anger because it's forced by the coin; then later he gets withdrawal symptoms but it feels unearned because he didn't choose to give up the coin).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure where you're getting that Mr. Exolia is angry from. Exolia's character is pretty consistently witty and upbeat until the coin is taken away. You are correct that the coin causes him to have withdraw symptoms when taken but that is intentional. It is supposed to kinda symbolize the feeling a gambler has when they lose everything and can no longer play the game. Thats essentially the idea behind this whole thing, its supposed to represent the dangers of gambling addiction.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The change of the transformation into a monster being implied to be non-permanent feels weird; though I'm still very unclear on what actually happens when someone is turned into a monster.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So yes, the change is non-permanent. Basically when the coin is bet on and lands on the anomalous side it turns the person who flipped it into a monster who then kills their opponent. Its the springing of a trap that was set up when the person tricked their victim into making a bet with them. Then once the victim is dead/eaten the monster turns back into the person. Its kinda similar to how Pennywise the clown is actually a crazy alien, but he only reveals that when he's devouring someone, then he goes back to being a clown. Once again, I think all of this comes off more complete in my sandbox.</p>

